// Closing an element, and the style stack.

// An end element: finish the run, close the paragraph if this was one, and
// drop the style this element contributed. The order matters -- the run being
// flushed was written in this element's style, so the pop comes last.
odt_shut() {
  odt_flush();
  odt_close();
  odt_pop();
} return void;

odt_close() {
  if(xml_name() == "text:p" || xml_name() == "text:h") {
    odt_end();
  }
} return void;

// A start element can change the style in force, so the run in progress ends
// here -- in the style it was written in, which is why the flush comes before
// the push. Every start element pushes, whether it names a style or not, so
// that every end element can pop without knowing what was pushed; an element
// with no text:style-name inherits the one already in force, which is what
// makes a text:span inside a text:span come out right.
//
// An *empty* element does neither, and that is the point: <text:s/> and
// <text:tab/> are part of the run they sit in, and flushing at one would split
// "a    b" into two runs that say the same thing in the same style.
odt_stack(int k) {
  if(k == 1) {
    odt_flush();
    push((import odt_stk), odt_style_now());
  }
} return void;
